Carafe: shared user data and thread data in a 32-bit address space.
A Win32 program without relocations needs its fixed low base, which only a process Horizon creates
with a 32-bit address space (NPDM type 2) has. There the kernel's heap region covers 0x7ffe0000 and
nothing lies above 4 GB. KUSER_SHARED_DATA moves to a free page below 4 GB when its address is taken,
as before Wine 11.18 (b231b265): PE ntdll, kernel32 and kernelbase find it through
wine_nx_user_shared_data, direct reads are redirected by horizon.c. Thread data takes the top of the
space instead of requiring addresses above 4 GB. Nothing changes in a 36- or 39-bit address space.
docs/research/fixed-base/REPORT.md, P1.
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the file it changes.

